WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:05.220
A preflight request is an automatic browser OPTIONS request.

2
00:00:05.320 --> 00:00:12.760
The browser uses it to ask the server whether a planned cross-origin request is allowed under the CORS policy before sending the actual request.

3
00:00:12.860 --> 00:00:18.940
This behavior is part of browser enforcement, not a separate feature the application user triggers.

4
00:00:19.220 --> 00:00:27.540
During preflight, the browser may send the Origin header, Access-Control-Request-Method, and Access-Control-Request-Headers.

5
00:00:27.640 --> 00:00:35.240
Those values describe the origin making the browser request, the method the browser wants to use, and the non-simple request headers it wants to include.

6
00:00:35.340 --> 00:00:39.580
The server response tells the browser whether that combination is allowed.

7
00:00:39.860 --> 00:00:45.220
Successful preflight does not authenticate the user and does not prove the frontend is trustworthy.

8
00:00:45.320 --> 00:00:51.080
It only tells the browser that the server permits the described cross-origin browser request under the policy.

9
00:00:51.180 --> 00:00:55.580
Authentication and authorization still happen through normal application controls.

10
00:00:55.680 --> 00:01:00.480
A clean preflight response should never be treated as proof that the action itself is allowed.

11
00:01:00.760 --> 00:01:07.640
APIs, reverse proxies, gateways, and hosting layers need to handle OPTIONS behavior consistently.

12
00:01:07.740 --> 00:01:14.060
A request may pass through infrastructure before it reaches the ASP.NET Core application.

13
00:01:14.160 --> 00:01:29.520
If one layer blocks OPTIONS, strips headers, rewrites paths, changes host information, or applies a different CORS policy, the browser may report a CORS error even though the underlying issue is ordinary configuration mismatch.

14
00:01:29.800 --> 00:01:32.200
Troubleshooting should stay methodical.

15
00:01:32.300 --> 00:01:44.780
Confirm the intended origin, method, request headers, credential mode, endpoint, route, ASP.NET Core middleware placement, endpoint metadata, and proxy behavior.

16
00:01:44.880 --> 00:01:48.080
Also confirm which environment is being tested.

17
00:01:48.180 --> 00:01:54.980
Many CORS problems come from a difference between local development, staging, production, and the edge infrastructure.

18
00:01:55.260 --> 00:02:00.700
The operational lesson is that a browser CORS error is a symptom, not a diagnosis.

19
00:02:00.800 --> 00:02:06.560
Treat it as a prompt to verify the complete path from browser request through gateway and application policy.

20
00:02:06.660 --> 00:02:14.180
Avoid broadening policy only to make the error disappear; first confirm what access is actually required.
